    "thm-first-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-for-l-one": {
      "id": "thm-first-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-for-l-one",
      "path": "items/thm-first-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-for-l-one.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]).\n\nLet $a<b$ and let $f\\in L^1([a,b])$. Define\n$$F(x):=\\int_a^x f(t)\\,dt\\qquad(a\\le x\\le b).$$\nThen $F$ is differentiable for almost every $x\\in(a,b)$ and\n$$F'(x)=f(x)$$\nat every such point.",
      "sha256": "5b200ddc6d51af8593ecf55669db2571e2d8f71cb81a79549fcf5efad02b0f5c",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
